  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Monday)\nIssued at 1235 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nClosed upper low is centered over southeastern OR with deep \ntrough axis extending southward off southern CA coast. Water vapor\nimagery shows shortwave trough pushing north of the forecast area\ninto ND with additional moisture and embedded vort maxes to our \nwest along the eastern periphery of the low. A surface cold front \nstretches from southern MN through northeastern NE back into \nsouthwestern NE. Low clouds with mist/fog persist across the \nforecast area early this afternoon, although breaks in the clouds \nare becoming more numerous and expansive. Cold tonight with many \nareas falling into the mid to upper 30s.\n\nThe upper low is forecast to dig into the southwestern US with the \ntrough axis shifting further east overnight tonight into Tuesday as \nbroad upper level ascent overspreads the region. Light rain possible \novernight tonight into Tuesday morning with ample cloud cover. \nAdditional chances of light precipitation arrive early Wednesday \nthrough Thursday morning, with the best chances for widespread \nprecipitation along the SD/NE border and southward.\n\nNorthern stream trough is forecast to drop out of Saskatchewan and \nManitoba into the Upper Midwest early Thursday, swinging a strong \ncold front through the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest. A strong \nbelt of 850-700 mb wind (~40-60 knots) will push into ND and central\nSD, sideswiping the forecast area. The strongest cold air \nadvection will remain displaced east of the forecast area, where \nstrongest winds can be expected. Deterministic NBM guidance \ncontinues to forecast 60+ mph wind gusts across portions of NW SD \nand the western SD plains Thursday afternoon. However, an \ningredients-based approach coupled with ensemble guidance does \nnot support winds this strong. The current forecast has Wind \nAdvisory level winds (~45 mph) for portions of NW SD into the \nwestern SD plains Thursday afternoon and evening, although there \nis low confidence that this solution verifies; certainly, the High\nWind (>60 mph) potential looks unlikely (probability < 15%).\n\nWestern ridging returns next weekend with above average temperatures \nforecast and dry conditions.",
